=== pigz
--- pigz -
--best 	compression level 9
--fast 	compression level 1
-0 	compression level 0 (-1 to -9, -11 are the others)
-A 	use NAME as the name for any --zip entry from stdin
-C 	put TEXT in the gzip or zip header
-F 	do iterations first, before block split for -11
-H 	use only Huffman coding for compression
-I 	number of iterations for -11 optimization
-J 	maximum number of split blocks for -11
-K 	compress to PKWare zip (.zip) single entry format
-L 	display the pigz license and quit
-M 	store or restore mod time
-N 	store or restore file name and mod time
-O 	do not split into smaller blocks for -11
-R 	input-determined block locations for rsync
-S 	use SUFFIX instead of .gz (for compression)
-U 	use run-length encoding for compression
-V 	show the version of pigz
-Y 	force output file write to permanent storage
-b 	set compression block size to KB kilobytes
-c 	write all processed output to stdout (won't delete)
-d 	decompress the compressed input
-f 	force overwrite, compress .gz, links, and to terminal
-h 	display a help screen and quit
-i 	compress blocks independently for damage recovery
-k 	do not delete original file after processing
-l 	list the contents of the compressed input
-m 	do not store or restore mod time
-n 	do not store or restore file name or mod time
-p 	allow up to N compression threads
-q 	print no messages, even on error
-r 	process the contents of all subdirectories
-t 	test the integrity of the compressed input
-v 	provide more verbose output
-z 	compress to zlib (.zz) instead of gzip format
--- pigz --su
--suffix=	use SUFFIX instead of .gz (for compression)
--- pigz -S 
.gz 
.zip 
.zz 
--- pigz arch
archive.tar 
=== gnuplot
--- gnuplot -
-V 	show the version
-c 	run the script FILE, with its arguments ARG1 ARG2 ...
-d 	ignore the initialization file
-e 	run the commands
-h 	show help
-p 	keep the plot windows open after the script ends
-s 	wait for the terminal to close before exiting
--- gnuplot -c 
bin/
plot.gp 
--- gnuplot pl
plot.gp 
